title absorption: one pool takes over another's name

# Off-of renames /a to /a2 and hard-links /b onto the same dnode, so
# two base pools become one. Onto is quiet, nothing conflicts.
#
# Two cases neither emission compiler had crossed:
#
#   ABSORPTION. The merged output pool reuses 6-100 -- /b's dnode --
#   and not 5-100, which is the pool whose CONTENT it takes. So the
#   surviving dnode and the surviving bytes come from different
#   places, and 5-100's name is unlinked because no output pool
#   reuses it.
#
#   A WRITE ON A MULTI-NAME POOL. The pool ends up holding both /a2
#   and /b on one dnode, so a write through either name is the same
#   write. Which name carries it is unforced, and this pins the
#   choice: the lowest-sorted decided name.
#
# The anchor is also visible here: /a2 links from /b, the KEPT name,
# which exists on the clone before the plan starts and so depends on
# nothing.

tree base
  1-1: /
    5-100: a = v0
    6-100: b = w0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    5-100: a = v0
    6-100: b = w0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    5-100: a2 = v0
    5-100: b
